# The release tree, as a versioned artifact (WO-44 · LACTEVA-DEPLOY-003).
#
# WHAT PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. `deploy.sh` used to build a new release by
# rsyncing the host's OWN current release into it. The code came from ECR at
# an exact tag, but everything the code runs BESIDE — the nginx configuration,
# the compose file, the backup and restore scripts, the deploy script itself —
# came from whatever happened to be on that disk. A pushed commit was
# therefore not deployable: `fe86d8c` fixed nginx's security headers, passed
# every gate, published both images, deployed cleanly, and the headers stayed
# missing on the live site, because nothing carried the file. The only route
# was a manual rsync over SSH from an allow-listed address.
#
# So the release tree becomes an artifact like the code: built once in CI from
# the commit, pushed to the registry the host already pulls from, addressed by
# the same tag. `main-abc1234` now names three images, and deploying that tag
# deploys that commit — configuration included.
#
# WHY AN IMAGE RATHER THAN GIT ON THE HOST. Fetching the commit on the host
# would be the shorter sentence, and it was rejected for two reasons. It needs
# a long-lived deploy key ON the host, when the pipeline's stated direction is
# the opposite — "no AWS secret is stored in GitHub", OIDC instead of keys,
# "the EC2 now only ever pulls". And it puts GitHub in the deploy path, so an
# outage there would block a deploy the registry could otherwise serve. ECR is
# already reachable with the instance profile and no secret at all.
#
# WHY THIS BASE. `python:3.13-slim-bookworm` is what the API image's runtime
# stage is built on, so the host has already pulled every one of these layers
# and the incremental download is the release tree itself — a few hundred
# kilobytes. A smaller base with different layers would cost MORE to pull.
FROM python:3.13-slim-bookworm

# CURATED, not the whole repository — which is what the rsync copied, and is
# the wrong default for an artifact that leaves this machine:
#
#   * `infra/terraform/` holds `terraform.tfstate`, and a state file carries
#     real values. It must not be baked into an image that anyone who can pull
#     from the registry can read. `.dockerignore` excludes it; this comment
#     exists so nobody "fixes" that later.
#   * `node_modules/` and `.venv/` are 1.7 GB of build output that the running
#     stack never reads.
#
# Everything the deployed platform actually touches is here: every path the
# compose file bind-mounts, every script `deploy.sh` and the systemd units
# invoke, and the seeder and restore tooling the operator runs by hand.
WORKDIR /release
COPY docker-compose.production.yml ./
COPY infra ./infra

# The compose file falls back to `./infra/nginx/certs` when TLS_CERT_DIR is
# unset. It is set on every real host (`/etc/lacteva/certs`), but the mount
# point has to exist for the fallback not to have Docker create it root-owned.
RUN mkdir -p /release/infra/nginx/certs

# Nothing runs. This image is a container to copy OUT of — `docker create`
# followed by `docker cp`, which is why it needs no entrypoint of its own but
# does need a command that `docker create` will accept.
CMD ["/bin/false"]
